{%- comment -%}
  oma-bloggle-skin — brand skin for the Bloggle app's article template.
  Renders no markup of its own; its only job is the {% style %} block below,
  which re-skins the fixed IDs/classes Bloggle's own section
  (main-bloggle-article.liquid, app-owned — see CLAUDE.md's ownership map,
  can't be edited directly) always renders.

  Settings-driven like every other oma-* section, rather than a static CSS
  file, so colors/width are adjustable from the theme editor without a code
  change.

  Two fixes this covers that plain font/color swaps didn't:
  - Bloggle's own CDN stylesheet (article-layout.min.css) makes
    #bloggle_article-layout a flex row: the article content takes
    `calc(100% - 210px)` when a related product is linked to make room for
    its sticky sidebar, otherwise the full width — up to ~1100px either way,
    with nothing capping the RTE's reading width. text_max_width below caps
    it and centers it.
  - The title, meta line (author/date/read time), RTE body, back-to-blog
    link and share row are independent block children with no shared
    column — each one independently gets the same max-width and is
    left-aligned (not centered) so their left edges line up with each
    other and with the card's own left padding edge.

  Every selector is doubled up with "#bloggle_article-layout" as a prefix so
  it outranks Bloggle's own same-selector rules on specificity alone,
  regardless of where this section sits in the template's section order.

  Styled to read as a sibling of oma-editorial (bordered card, offset
  shadow, Sunbrella accent by default) rather than a from-scratch look —
  but note the adaptation: oma-editorial's card floats over a separate
  full-bleed sticky background photo with no image inside the card itself;
  Bloggle's structure puts the article's own image at the top of the same
  flex container as the text, so here the image sits inside the bordered
  card instead. There's no DOM hook to pull it out into a separate layer.

  Known gap: Bloggle's comment form isn't in main-bloggle-article.liquid's
  source — it's injected client-side by their CDN script
  (lazysizes-bloggle.min.js) with class names this repo can't see. Not
  styled here; check a review theme once comments are visible before adding
  rules for it.
{%- endcomment -%}
{% style %}
{%- comment -%}
  The id is doubled (#bloggle_article-layout#bloggle_article-layout) to
  double its specificity against Bloggle's own same-id rule, which sets
  width/max-width with !important in two places (its CDN stylesheet and
  its own inline <style>). Equal specificity would fall back to source
  order, which depends on where this section sits relative to "blog" in
  the template's section list — doubling the id wins outright instead, so
  this doesn't silently break again if that order changes.
{%- endcomment -%}
#bloggle_article-layout#bloggle_article-layout {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  max-width: {{ section.settings.card_max_width }}px !important;
  width: 100% !important;
  margin-left: auto !important;
  margin-right: auto !important;
  margin-top: {{ section.settings.card_margin_top }}px !important;
  margin-bottom: {{ section.settings.card_margin_bottom }}px !important;
  background: {{ section.settings.card_background }};
  border: 1.5px solid {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  box-shadow: 8px 8px 0 {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  padding: {{ section.settings.card_padding_top }}px clamp(20px, 5vw, 64px) {{ section.settings.card_padding_bottom }}px;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle--blog-item-img {
  border-radius: 0;
}

{%- comment -%}
  Bloggle's "text__center" utility class on #article-header isn't just
  text-align:center -- it's display:flex;flex-direction:column;align-items
  :center (confirmed in their CDN stylesheet, article-layout.min.css). That
  makes #article-header a flex container that horizontally CENTERS its
  children as flex items -- the title and the meta row (.info_header) --
  regardless of text-align. The title looks unaffected because it's nearly
  as wide as its own column already, so centering it is invisible; the much
  narrower meta row visibly floats in the middle. text-align:left alone
  never fixed this because text-align only affects inline content inside a
  box, not a flex item's own position -- align-items is the property that
  actually controls it, overridden explicitly below.
{%- endcomment -%}
#bloggle_article-layout #article-header {
  text-align: left;
  align-items: flex-start;
  max-width: {{ section.settings.text_max_width }}px;
  margin-left: 0;
  margin-right: auto;
}

#bloggle_article-layout #bloggle--article-title {
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  font-weight: 900;
  font-size: clamp(32px, 5vw, 56px);
  line-height: 1.05;
  letter-spacing: -0.03em;
  color: {{ section.settings.heading_color }};
  text-wrap: balance;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .info_header {
  display: flex;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: flex-start;
  gap: 4px 10px;
  margin-top: 14px;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .info_header #bloggle--blog-item-author,
#bloggle_article-layout .info_header #bloggle--blog-item-ttread {
  margin: 0;
  font-family: var(--font-body-family);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  line-height: 1.55;
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
}

#bloggle_article-layout .info_header #separator {
  margin: 0;
  color: {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  opacity: 0.6;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte,
#bloggle_article-layout #bloggle_rte {
  max-width: {{ section.settings.text_max_width }}px;
  margin-left: 0;
  margin-right: auto;
  font-family: var(--font-body-family);
}

{%- comment -%}
  margin is bottom-only (zeroed on first-child, zeroed on last-child's
  bottom) rather than top-and-bottom -- adjacent paragraphs would normally
  margin-collapse down to a single gap anyway, but a bottom-only margin
  guarantees every gap is exactly paragraph_spacing regardless of what
  markup the rich text editor happens to produce (e.g. an empty <p><br></p>
  for a blank line still only adds one more of the same fixed amount,
  rather than two different top+bottom values compounding unevenly).
{%- endcomment -%}
#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte p:not(.bggle-font) {
  font-size: 16px;
  line-height: {{ section.settings.text_line_height }};
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
  margin: 0 0 {{ section.settings.paragraph_spacing }}px;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte p:not(.bggle-font):first-child {
  margin-top: 0;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte p:not(.bggle-font):last-child {
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte a {
  color: {{ section.settings.link_color }};
  text-decoration-color: {{ section.settings.link_color }};
  text-underline-offset: 3px;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte a:hover {
  opacity: 0.8;
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte blockquote {
  margin: 28px 0;
  padding: 4px 0 4px 20px;
  border-left: 3px solid {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
  font-style: italic;
  color: {{ section.settings.heading_color }};
}

#bloggle_article-layout .bloggle_rte img {
  border-radius: 0;
}

#bloggle_article-layout #bloggle--blog-item-goback {
  max-width: {{ section.settings.text_max_width }}px;
  margin-left: 0;
  margin-right: auto;
  margin-top: 32px;
}

#bloggle_article-layout #bloggle--blog-item-goback a {
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  font-weight: 800;
  font-size: 12px;
  letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  color: {{ section.settings.link_color }};
  text-decoration: none;
}

#bloggle_article-layout #bloggle--blog-item-goback a:hover {
  opacity: 0.8;
}

#bloggle_article-layout #share-buttons {
  max-width: {{ section.settings.text_max_width }}px;
  margin-left: 0;
  margin-right: auto;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: flex-start;
  align-items: center;
  gap: 20px;
  margin-top: 28px;
  padding-top: 20px;
  border-top: 1.5px solid {{ section.settings.accent_color }};
}

#bloggle_article-layout #share-buttons a {
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 12.5px;
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }};
  text-decoration: none;
}

#bloggle_article-layout #share-buttons a:hover {
  opacity: 0.75;
}

/* The share icons carry inline style="fill:#000000" on the <svg>/<path>
   elements themselves (vendor markup, not editable) — only an !important
   external rule can out-rank an inline style attribute. */
#bloggle_article-layout #share-buttons svg,
#bloggle_article-layout #share-buttons svg path {
  fill: {{ section.settings.link_color }} !important;
}

#bloggle_sideBar {
  font-family: var(--font-body-family);
}

#bloggle_sideBar .bggle--product-info,
#bloggle_sideBar p,
#bloggle_sideBar li {
  color: {{ section.settings.text_color }} !important;
}

#bloggle_sideBar .bggle--product-title {
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  font-weight: 800;
}

#bloggle_sideBar .bggle--product-vendor {
  font-family: var(--font-heading-family);
  color: {{ section.settings.link_color }};
}
{% endstyle %}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "OMA Bloggle Skin",
  "tag": "section",
  "disabled_on": {
    "groups": ["header", "footer"]
  },
  "settings": [
    { "type": "header", "content": "Card" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "card_background", "label": "Card background", "default": "#FFFFFF" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "accent_color", "label": "Accent (card border & shadow, blockquote rule)", "info": "Border and shadow always match each other, same convention as OMA Editorial.", "default": "#FBB925" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "card_max_width", "label": "Max card width", "min": 480, "max": 1100, "step": 20, "unit": "px", "default": 800, "info": "Matches OMA Editorial's own \"Max Card Width\" default." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "card_padding_top", "label": "Card padding top (inside, border to title)", "min": 24, "max": 160, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "default": 40 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "card_padding_bottom", "label": "Card padding bottom (inside, share row to border)", "min": 24, "max": 160, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "default": 72 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "card_margin_top", "label": "Space above card (outside, section to border)", "min": 0, "max": 160, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "default": 48 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "card_margin_bottom", "label": "Space below card (outside, border to next section)", "min": 0, "max": 160, "step": 4, "unit": "px", "default": 56 },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Colors" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "heading_color", "label": "Title & blockquote color", "default": "#00180C" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "text_color", "label": "Body text color", "default": "#00180C" },
    { "type": "color", "id": "link_color", "label": "Link / share / back-to-blog color", "default": "#FF3F00" },
    { "type": "header", "content": "Layout" },
    { "type": "range", "id": "text_max_width", "label": "Reading column width", "min": 480, "max": 900, "step": 20, "unit": "px", "default": 720, "info": "Shared by the title, meta line, body text, back-to-blog link and share row, so their left edges line up. Left-aligned within the card, not centered." },
    { "type": "range", "id": "text_line_height", "label": "Body line height", "min": 1.2, "max": 2, "step": 0.1, "default": 1.5 },
    { "type": "range", "id": "paragraph_spacing", "label": "Space between paragraphs", "min": 0, "max": 48, "step": 2, "unit": "px", "default": 22, "info": "Bottom margin only, so every gap between paragraphs is identical regardless of what the rich text editor outputs." }
  ],
  "presets": [
    { "name": "OMA Bloggle Skin" }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
